  "query_preview": "..(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 349 PM EDT SAT OCT 10 2026\n\nThe extended period opens Monday morning with the remnant low \nfrom Isaias departing the area towards the northeast into the \ncoastal Mid-Atlantic and Southern New England. A few residual \nlight showers across the far eastern fringes of the Jackson CWA \nearly Monday morning gives way to a strong upper-level ridge. An \nunseasonably warm and dry start to the extended forecast period \nacross eastern Kentucky will yield to an active weather pattern by\nmid-week, marked by a strong cold front, rising precipitation \nchances, and a sharp autumn cool- down for the weekend.\n\nThe strong upper-level ridge centered across the Central US and \nOhio Valley, keeps eastern Kentucky under subsidence with \ndecreasing clouds through the day. 500 mb heights running in the \n80th to 95th percentile for mid-October will push daytime \ntemperatures well above seasonal normals through Thursday, \ngenerally ranging 5 to 10 degrees above normal in the upper 70s to\nlow 80s. Tuesdays forecast high temperature of 82 at the JKL \nairport is within 2 degrees of the established record high of 84, \nset back in 1985. In fact, the 00Z LREF ensemble shows a 35 to 40 \npercent chance of reaching 84 degrees.\n\nModel soundings from Monday and Tuesday afternoons show steep\nlow-level lapse rates from the surface to 850 mb with a\npronounced dry layer above the boundary layer. To account for\nthis boundary layer mixing, raw model guidance was blended with\nthe deterministic NBM to slightly lower dew points both\nafternoons. Through the day Tuesday, the pressure gradient\nacross the area increases ahead of an approaching system from\nthe Central Plains. This increased gradient could lead to breezy\nconditions in the Bluegrass and areas along and west of I-75\nTuesday afternoon, where wind gusts of 15 to 20 mph are\npossible. While warm, dry, and a little breezy, fire weather\nshould not be of too much concern as min RH remains in the\nupper 30s to mid 40s through the day.\n\nHigh pressure anchored off the Southeast coast will maintain warm\nadvection through mid-week. However, a weak surface boundary\nsagging south across the Ohio Valley will bring low-end rain\nchances to eastern Kentucky Wednesday afternoon and evening.\n\nA more significant pattern shift occurs Thursday as a cut-off\nlow over the Four Corners ejects northeastward toward the\nNorthern Plains and Upper Midwest. As the boundary lifts back\nnorth as a warm front, a strengthening southerly low-level jet\nwill tap directly into Gulf moisture, advecting some across the \nCommonwealth. Model and ensemble agreement begins to drop toward \nthe second half of the extended period. It will be interesting to \nsee how this cutoff low from the Four Corners interacts with the \nnorthern stream as it approaches the Upper Midwest and Great Lakes\nThursday onward. Due to the uncertainty, the NBM deterministic \nhas maintained smoothed-out isolated to scattered PoPs across the \narea Thursday and Friday. Some ensembles are showing PWATs across \neastern Kentucky climbing from around 1.0 inch early Thursday to \n1.4 inches by Thursday evening and Friday morning - representing \nan anomalous moisture plume near the 90th percentile for mid- \nOctober. While the present forecast doesn't have heavy rainfall \ndue to model uncertainty, the anomalous moisture plume in some \nensembles hints that heavy rainfall near the end of the week \nremains a possibility.\n\nA cold front associated with this late-week system could pass\nthrough eastern Kentucky during the day Friday. Behind the front,\ncold, dry continental polar air is modeled to intrude into the\nOhio Valley Friday evening and Saturday. PWAT steadily falls\nthrough Saturday, modeled by some as low as 0.2 to 0.4 inches.\nTemperatures that were once 5 to 10 degrees above normal could\nswing 5 to 10 degrees below normal by next Saturday.",
